{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792310642,"nonce":"oQHT2uu5Y4DbiMhZBcbuYQVDbqU=","title":"FIXED in 9d713ba6 — the Maximum tier used to compile inline in execute_function, stalling the program; it now compiles on beadie's broker ...","message":"**THE CAUSE of the 2026-08-23 CI 2x** (binarytrees 179 → 371, fibonacci and\nnbody likewise). Fixed in 6caafa05 by putting `TierPreset::Benchmark`'s\n`blazing_threshold` back to `u64::MAX`.\n\nMeasured on the Mac, within-run ratio (absolutes drift — the box was\ncontended, so only same-run ratios are meaningful):\n\n| threshold | tiered ÷ cranelift, fibonacci |\n|---|---|\n| 10 | **2.76x** |\n| u64::MAX | **1.06x** |\n\n**Why reaching the tier costs.** Maximum is the one tier **beadie does not\nown** — it handles Standard and Optimized only. `record_call` queues Maximum\nand `execute_function` **drains that queue inline, before running the\nfunction**, so the program stops for the entire LLVM compile and pays for it\ninside the measured window. `queue_ahead` is **not used anywhere** in\n`compiler/src`; the adapter is built with a bare `ThresholdPolicy::new(n)`.\n\nOSR does not rescue it: the resume points are built by that same blocking\ndrain, so the code a back edge would transfer into does not exist until after\nthe stall is paid.\n\nIt compounds with [[project_per_function_llvm_promotion]]: only the ENTRY is\never counted hot, so what the program stalls to compile is `main`, whose\ncallees stay on Cranelift. Maximum cost, near-zero change to what executes.\n\n**The mistake to not repeat:** per-function compile made the compile SMALLER,\nnot ASYNCHRONOUS, and lowering the threshold on the strength of \"promotion is\ncheap now\" ignored that a smaller stall is still a stall.\n\n**FIXED in 9d713ba6.** Maximum now rides beadie exactly like the Cranelift\ntiers: same `BackendAdapter\u003cBeadieJit\u003e` type, same bead registry, same\npoll-and-install path, selected by `beadie_jit::LLVM_OPT_LEVEL` (\"llvm\")\ninstead of a cranelift opt setting. `BeadieJit::compile` branches to\n`tiered_backend::compile_llvm_bead` for that level. Nothing about routing,\npolling or installation is particular to it, which is why this is small.\n\n**The main-thread claim was FALSE.** `record_call` said Maximum \"must run on\nthe main thread because of LLVM's `add_global_mapping` constraint\". It does\nnot, and the comment was inherited rather than tested. Beadie carries the work\nas an `Arc\u003cBead\u003e` with the modules behind `Arc\u003cRwLock\u003cVec\u003cIrModule\u003e\u003e\u003e` — the\ndifficulty it had already solved — and the context, module and engine are built\non the broker thread and left mapped there. Only an address comes back.\n\nMeasured, promotion on vs off, same build (Mac, aarch64):\n\n| bench | off | on |\n|---|---|---|\n| nbody | 658.80 | **510.80** |\n| fibonacci | 302.96 | **272.55** |\n| mandelbrot | 336.78 | **327.79** |\n| binarytrees | 309.14 | 313.10 |\n| deltablue | 31.54 | **35.76 (worse)** |\n\nnbody lands UNDER `rayzor-llvm`'s own figure — it skips the whole-program\ncompile that target pays up front.\n\n**deltablue is the informative one.** At ~31 ms it never recovers a ~23 ms\ncompile competing for a core. Off the critical path is not free. A call count\nalone cannot tell that from nbody: **ten calls in 30 ms is not the same heat as\nten calls in 500 ms**, so a threshold that decides this needs a time component.\n\nSTILL OPEN before lowering `blazing_threshold` (deliberately left at\n`u64::MAX`; `RAYZOR_BLAZING_THRESHOLD` overrides it for measurement):\n- Inner-function profiling. Only the entry is ever counted, so even a free\n  promotion compiles `main` alone while its callees stay on Cranelift — the\n  remaining gap to what LLVM could give.\n- A heat measure that accounts for elapsed time, so short programs do not\n  promote into a compile they cannot amortise.\n\nThe tier's value is architecture-dependent: on the x86_64 NUC, LLVM measured\n*slower* than Cranelift on fib.\n\n---\nArchived from Claude memory `bugs_llvm_tier_stalls_to_compile` on 2026-10-18. A record of past work; open items in it may have their own issues.","files":null}]}